// Type checking for what the extension ships — `npm run lint:types`, no build step and no
// transpilation: the file that is checked is the file that is published.
//
// Two configurations, because the extension has two global scopes and they are not the same
// one. This is the pages' scope: the popup, the options page and the content script. They load
// i18n.js and ui.js as classic scripts and share their declarations, exactly as the ESLint
// configuration describes. The service worker is jsconfig.worker.json — it has no `document`,
// and it declares names of its own that collide with the pages' when both are read as one.
//
// `strict` stays off, deliberately. Turning it on means a JSDoc type on every parameter of
// every function, sixteen files of it, for what it catches here: nothing the agents have ever
// got wrong. What is caught without it is what we do get wrong — a property that does not
// exist on a DOM node or on a `chrome.*` answer, a call with the wrong number of arguments, a
// value used as a number that is not one. Tightening it later is a configuration line, plus
// the annotations it then asks for.
{
  "compilerOptions": {
    "checkJs": true,
    "noEmit": true,
    "target": "es2022",
    "lib": ["es2022", "dom", "dom.iterable"],
    "strict": false,
    // @types/chrome only: nothing here runs on Node.
    "types": ["chrome"]
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*.js", "types/*.d.ts"],
  "exclude": ["src/background.js"]
}
